WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.310
When a Transformer reads a long sequence, each query can compare
itself with every key.

00:00:05.310 --> 00:00:08.971
Double the sequence length, and the number of scores quadruples.

00:00:08.971 --> 00:00:14.524
A conventional implementation writes that whole grid to GPU
memory, then reads it for softmax.

00:00:14.524 --> 00:00:17.941
What if we could get the same answer without ever saving the
grid?

00:00:18.591 --> 00:00:25.399
The large box is HBM, the GPU's main memory. It holds the
queries, keys, and values.

00:00:25.399 --> 00:00:30.581
The smaller box is on-chip SRAM. It is much faster, but there
is far less of it.

00:00:30.581 --> 00:00:35.774
On the A100, the paper estimates about nineteen
terabytes per second for on-chip memory.

00:00:35.774 --> 00:00:39.691
Main memory delivers one point five to two terabytes per second.

00:00:40.341 --> 00:00:44.896
Take a block of keys and values into fast memory. Bring in a
block of queries.

00:00:44.896 --> 00:00:47.918
Their dot products fill one tile of the score grid.

00:00:47.918 --> 00:00:52.880
While that tile is still on chip, compute exponentials and
combine them with the values.

00:00:52.880 --> 00:00:57.783
Reuse the key and value block with more queries, then move to
the next key block.

00:00:57.783 --> 00:01:02.477
The grid maps the work. It never needs to exist as a full array
in main memory.

00:01:03.127 --> 00:01:09.261
Softmax divides each exponential by the sum across its entire
row. A tile sees only part of that row.

00:01:09.261 --> 00:01:14.640
Keep a running maximum, a sum of exponentials, and a weighted
sum of values for each query.

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:20.031
When a new score raises the maximum, rescale both old sums to
use the new reference.

00:01:20.031 --> 00:01:25.062
Add the new tile's contributions, and divide the weighted sum by
the exponential sum.

00:01:25.062 --> 00:01:29.838
After the last tile, this is the same mathematical result as
full softmax.

00:01:30.488 --> 00:01:34.161
Training also needs attention probabilities in the backward
pass.

00:01:34.161 --> 00:01:37.833
Keep the output and a small normalization summary for each row.

00:01:37.833 --> 00:01:42.179
Reload the inputs and rebuild the probability tiles as gradients
need them.

00:01:42.179 --> 00:01:48.592
The extra arithmetic buys fewer trips to main memory. The saved
attention state grows linearly with sequence length.

00:01:49.242 --> 00:01:57.152
The paper reports up to three times faster attention than
PyTorch and up to twenty times better memory efficiency than
exact baselines.

00:01:57.152 --> 00:02:02.241
BERT large reached its training target in seventeen point four
minutes, compared with twenty.

00:02:02.241 --> 00:02:07.830
GPT-2 medium trained in six point nine days, compared with
twenty-one for Hugging Face.

00:02:07.830 --> 00:02:12.861
Dense FlashAttention reached sixty-one point four percent on
Path-X at sixteen K.

00:02:12.861 --> 00:02:18.669
The block-sparse variant reached sixty-three point one percent
on Path-256 at sixty-four K.

00:02:19.319 --> 00:02:21.889
Dense attention still does pairwise work.

00:02:21.889 --> 00:02:27.431
Change where the intermediate results live, and the same
calculation can become much cheaper to run.
